WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:05.300
I help some garden lovers
with their deployment times.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.102
Are we potting fake plants?

00:00:07.102 --> 00:00:08.060
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.210
Rainmages is a company
that has a robust back end

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.480
system with lots of small
microservices, each one powered

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.220
by Google Cloud Functions.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.050
They ran into an issue.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.420
Doing 200 or so code
deployments a day,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.430
their team was wasting
a lot of time waiting

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.230
for code to be moved around.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.180
Digging in, one of the
first things we noticed

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.240
was that each GCF
for each microservice

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.610
had a different set
of dependencies.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.010
And where they were using
the same dependency,

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.220
chances are they weren't
using the same versions.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.240
This threw a massive red flag.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.330
As versions of each dependency
vary across deployments,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.700
dependency cache misses
are much more likely,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.070
meaning the deployment
times will be longer.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.930
We were able to unify
the instances to use

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.571
all of the same
versions of a library,

00:00:52.571 --> 00:00:55.070
meaning they were more likely
to be in the dependency cache,

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.110
making deployments
a little faster.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.720
Second, I noticed a few
interesting statistics

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.540
with respect to their
deployment setup.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.040
It looked as though they were
shipping a lot of extra files,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.460
around 600 or so extra
config and data files.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.020
They would then fetch these
files from the local GCS bucket

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.640
that their function
stuff was deployed into.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.830
While this makes managing
microservice deployments

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.260
easier, it was killing
their deployment times.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.060
Instead, we pushed
their extra data

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.370
to a separate cloud
storage bucket,

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.020
so that uploads can
happen in parallel

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.630
and were shared in
some cases, resulting

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.402
in another great
performance boost.

00:01:28.402 --> 00:01:30.110
If you'd like to know
more about our work

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.240
to fix deployment times,
check out the article

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.536
linked the description below.

00:01:33.536 --> 00:01:35.660
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.099
Performance Atlas content.

00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:38.640
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.800
every millisecond counts.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

